rred caramelized outside  and this really juicy tender inside you  shave that layer off and then you're  giving another layer a chance to set up  on the outside  people who know Alpesh store in the  United States tend to think the identity  of that taco is pork plus pineapple when  it's not what makes an al pastor taco is  that it's been spit roasted vertically  we put a nicotine Apple because that's  sweet acidic contrast to the pork to me  as a no-brainer a little bit of cilantro  and onion  and a red sauce and a green sauce you  get this sort of traffic light  chromatography on the taco that I love  those salsas the pineapple the fresh  cilantro  that's sort of what cleanses the palate  it's all in the taco right there it's  almost like a three-course meal all in  one my first real al pastor taco and my  favorite is in a tiny little kiosk  called el wa kitto in Mexico City when  you take a look at ours it'll be very  different than that that's out of  respect I think there's being inspired  by something and being motivated by  something and copying it and that's not  what we do people are very concerned  about authenticity and tradition when it  comes to this cuisine  I love the al pastor taco in that way  because it's a shining example of how  untraditional this cuisine is in many  ways and how the greatness of it  actually came from radical change and  creativity  [Music]  you  [Music]

